      "notes": [
        "**Подобатися flips the sentence around.** *Мені подобається Київ* literally means \"to me, Kyiv is pleasing\". The subject is **Київ**, not **я**. That's why \"I\" goes into the dative (*мені*, *тобі*, *йому*) and never appears as *я*. English once worked the same way: *it pleases me*.",
        "**So the verb agrees with the thing liked, not the person.** *Мені подобається музика* (one thing) versus *Мені подобаються люди* (several). ⚠️ This is the most common beginner mistake: using *подобається* with a plural thing.",
        "**Любити takes знахідний (accusative), like an ordinary verb.** *Я люблю музику*: here \"I\" really is the subject, and the music is the object. The two sentences mean almost the same, but their structures run in opposite directions.",
        "**When to use which?** *Любити* is deeper and more lasting: feelings, passions, people. *Подобатися* is lighter and often about an impression: something you've just seen, tried, or visited. ⚠️ *Я люблю тебе* is a declaration of love; *Ти мені подобаєшся* means \"I have a crush on you\". Two sentences you must not mix up.",
        "**After любити, the second verb stays in its basic form.** *Люблю читати*, *люблю готувати*, *люблю слухати музику*. The second verb doesn't change at all: one easy thing in a language where so much changes.",
        "**Мені не подобається is the polite way to say you don't like something.** Because the sentence is flipped, it sounds like \"it doesn't appeal to me\", not an attack on the thing itself. It's how Ukrainians turn down food, films, or suggestions without giving offense.",
        "**Вибач (to a friend) and Вибачте (polite).** Notice that Ani uses the short form with Andriy: they've been on *ти* terms since Unit 3. Saying *Вибачте* to a close friend sounds cold, as if you're keeping your distance."
      ],
